<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We give the argument for colimits; the case of limits follows by a dual argument. Let $p: K \rightarrow S$ be a (small) diagram in $S$. By Proposition <a href="/tag/3D6E95" data-tag="3D6E95">4.2.3.14</a>, there exists a (small) category $\calI $ and a cofinal map $q: \Nerve (\calI ) \rightarrow K$. Since $q$ is cofinal, $p$ has a colimit in $S$ if and only if $p \circ q$ has a colimit in $S$; thus we may reduce to the case where $K = \Nerve (\calI )$. 
    <p>
      Using Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a>, we may suppose that $p$ is the nerve of a injectively fibrant diagram $p': \calI \rightarrow {\mathbf A}^{\circ }$. Let $\overline{p'}: \calI \star \{ x\}  \rightarrow {\mathbf A}^{\calI }$ be a limit of $p'$, so that $\overline{p}'$ is a homotopy limit diagram in ${\mathbf A}$. Now choose a trivial fibration $\overline{p}'' \rightarrow \overline{p}'$ in ${\mathbf A}^{\calI }$, where $\overline{p}''$ is cofibrant. The simplicial nerve of $\overline{p}''$ determines a colimit diagram $\overline{f}: \Nerve (\calI )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow S$, by Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a>. We now observe that $f = \overline{f} | \Nerve (\calI )$ is equivalent to $p$, so that $p$ also admits a colimit in $S$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
